sections.content:
Voditelj pita postoji li način da se djecu sačuva od sadržaja koji se, prema njegovim riječima, protive svakom svjetonazoru i logici, a nude se kroz zdravstveni odgoj i školski sustav.

Josipa Nemet odgovara da je situacija u Hrvatskoj zasad povoljnija nego drugdje. Sporni program je **izvannastavna aktivnost**, pa roditelj mora dati suglasnost. Oni koji se tom temom bave moraju aktivno upozoravati roditelje da suglasnost ne daju i da djecu ne upisuju u takve aktivnosti. Ipak, najvažnijom smatra **roditeljsku budnost**.

Zakoni u Hrvatskoj, objašnjava, još koliko-toliko štite pravo roditelja da odlučuju o vrijednosnom odgoju djece, pa i o vjerskim uvjerenjima. Problem zato nije u tome da roditelji ne bi htjeli reagirati, nego u tome što **nisu budni** i misle da opasnost ne postoji. Iako štetni kurikulumi zasad nisu nacionalni, Nemet upozorava na sljedeće:

- razne **udruge ulaze na satove razrednika** na temelju ugovora sa školama;
- osnivač većine škola je **Grad Zagreb**, pa takvi ugovori lako prolaze na školskim odborima;
- na tim satovima provode se dijelovi spornog kurikuluma;
- roditelji često ne dobiju ni obavijest ni zahtjev za suglasnost, ili tek e-poruku da se jave ako se ne slažu.

Gošća razumije da su roditelji zauzeti borbom za egzistenciju. Ipak, svaki će na pitanje što mu je najvažnije odgovoriti: dijete. Na njega misli kad se probudi i kad ide spavati. Tijekom dana, međutim, djeluje duboka biološka **sklonost vjerovanju da zajednica želi dobro**. Roditelj i dijete prirodno očekuju da je u školi sve dobro. U modernom kontekstu to, upozorava, više ne vrijedi, a za deset godina vrijedit će još manje ako se društvo nastavi kretati istim smjerom. Katoličke škole od takvih su sadržaja zaštićene, dodaje.

Voditelj tu ubacuje kratko „za sad“, podsjećajući na Francusku, gdje je slučaj drugačiji.

sections.content_en:
The host asks whether there is a way to protect children from content that, in his words, goes against every worldview and logic, and is offered through health education and the school system.

Josipa Nemet responds that the situation in Croatia is currently more favorable than elsewhere. The controversial program is an **extracurricular activity**, so a parent must give consent. Those who deal with this topic must actively warn parents not to give consent and not to enroll children in such activities. Nevertheless, she considers **parental vigilance** to be the most important.

The laws in Croatia, she explains, still somewhat protect parents' right to decide on the values-based upbringing of their children, including religious beliefs. The problem, therefore, is not that parents would not want to react, but that they **are not vigilant** and think that danger does not exist. Although harmful curricula are not currently national, Nemet warns of the following:

- various **associations enter homeroom classes** based on contracts with schools;
- the founder of most schools is the **City of Zagreb**, so such contracts easily pass through school boards;
- parts of the controversial curriculum are implemented in those classes;
- parents often receive neither a notification nor a request for consent, or merely an email to reach out if they do not agree.

The guest understands that parents are busy struggling to make a living. Nevertheless, when asked what is most important to them, every parent will answer: the child. They think of the child when waking up and when going to sleep. During the day, however, a deep biological **tendency to believe that the community wishes well** is at work. Parent and child naturally expect that everything is fine at school. In a modern context, she warns, this no longer holds true, and in ten years it will hold even less true if society continues to move in the same direction. Catholic schools are protected from such content, she adds.

The host inserts a brief “for now” here, recalling France, where the case is different.
